<Line Number="5">
<![CDATA[1000 dints take around 500 ms of processing time (5555 controller) to sort with this routine.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="6">
<![CDATA[Make sure your process is not at some time critical point when sorting large amounts of data]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="7">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="8">
<![CDATA[Note at this time this routine does not properly handle the CST rolloever to a negative number....when bit]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="9">
<![CDATA[32 of the CST turns on as the clock increments, there may some cases where sorting would be wrong because a ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="10">
<![CDATA[negative CST is sorted against a positive CST when in reality the negative CST is a later time.  See the ladder version]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="11">
<![CDATA[of this sort for how to inspect bit .31 of CST*/ ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="12">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="13">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="14">
<![CDATA[  ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="15">
<![CDATA[  //move the data into a UDT that we will sort]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="16">
<![CDATA[ COP(SOE_Fifo_ExtractData[0],SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[0],SOE_StoreIndex) ;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="17">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="18">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="19">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="20">
<![CDATA[for Sort_I := 0 to (SOE_StoreIndex-2)  do;  // Bubble sort always goes from I= 0 to (max-1), but our index is actually one higher than the last element in the array so -2]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="21">
<![CDATA[	for Sort_J := 0 to (SOE_StoreIndex-2 - Sort_I) do;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="22">
<![CDATA[		]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="23">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="24">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="25">
<![CDATA[			//check if uppper 32 bit's are different... if so, copy]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="26">
<![CDATA[			if ( SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)].CSTTimeStamp[1] < SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)].CSTTimeStamp[1] ) then]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="27">
<![CDATA[				    //copy to temp]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="28">
<![CDATA[					cop(SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)], SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_temp, 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="29">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="30">
<![CDATA[					//copy j+ 1 to j]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="31">
<![CDATA[					cop (SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)], SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)], 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="32">
<![CDATA[	]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="33">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="34">
<![CDATA[					//copy  temp to j+1]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="35">
<![CDATA[					cop(SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_temp,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)], 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="36">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="37">
<![CDATA[				//check if lower 32 bit's are different....if so, copy]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="38">
<![CDATA[				else if ( SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)].CSTTimeStamp[0] < SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)].CSTTimeStamp[0] ) then]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="39">
<![CDATA[				    	//copy to temp]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="40">
<![CDATA[						cop(SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)], SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_temp, 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="41">
<![CDATA[			]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="42">
<![CDATA[						]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="43">
<![CDATA[						//copy j+ 1 to j]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="44">
<![CDATA[						cop (SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)], SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J)], 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="45">
<![CDATA[	]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="46">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="47">
<![CDATA[						//copy  temp to j+1]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="48">
<![CDATA[						cop(SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_temp,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[(Sort_J+1)], 1);]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="49">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="50">
<![CDATA[				end_if;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="51">
<![CDATA[			end_if;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="52">
<![CDATA[	]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="53">
<![CDATA[	end_for;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="54">
<![CDATA[end_for;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="55">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
</STContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="InsertionSort" Type="ST">
<STContent>
<Line Number="0">
<![CDATA[/* This is a sample insertion sort done in structured text]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="1">
<![CDATA[pass this routine the array size to sort (Array_Size) and it will sort]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="2">
<![CDATA[the array numbers (numbers[x]) ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="3">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="4">
<![CDATA[Use caution when executing sort routines on your contorl system.  ]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="5">
<![CDATA[1000 dints take around 400 ms of processing time (5555 controller) to sort with this routine.]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="6">
<![CDATA[Make sure your process is not at some time critical point when sorting large amounts of data*/]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="7">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="8">
<![CDATA[For I := 1 to Array_Size - 1 Do //outer loop]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="9">
<![CDATA[	Temp := numbers[I];]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="10">
<![CDATA[	for J := I to 1 by -1 Do  //inner looop]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="11">
<![CDATA[		if (numbers [J - 1] > Temp) then  //check for sort condition]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="12">
<![CDATA[			numbers[J] := numbers [J-1]; //switch positions in array if condition is met]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="13">
<![CDATA[		else]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="14">
<![CDATA[			exit;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="15">
<![CDATA[		end_if;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="16">
<![CDATA[	end_for;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="17">
<![CDATA[	numbers[J] :=Temp;  //return temp to numbers[j]]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="18">
<![CDATA[end_for;]]>
</Line>
<Line Number="19">
<![CDATA[]]>
</Line>
</STContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="SOE_AliasData" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Every SOE module you add to the system needs this rung to copy it's I/O data over to my array so it can be indirect addressed  later.  The branch in this routine also executes the EventAck in the event that the ExtractFifo routine has already read this piece of data.

This rung is for the SOE module in slot 2 of the local rack]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[COP(Local:2:I,SOE_DataTemp[0],1) ,MOV(SOE_LastDataValue[0],Local:2:O.EventAck) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Every SOE module you add to the system needs this rung to copy it's I/O data over to my array so it can be indirect addressed  later.  The branch in this routine also executes the EventAck in the event that the ExtractFifo routine has already read this piece of data.

This rung is for the SOE module in slot 3 of the local rack]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[COP(Local:3:I,SOE_DataTemp[1],1) ,MOV(SOE_LastDataValue[1],Local:3:O.EventAck) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Every SOE module you add to the system needs this rung to copy it's I/O data over to my array so it can be indirect addressed  later.  The branch in this routine also executes the EventAck in the event that the ExtractFifo routine has already read this piece of data.

This rung is for the SOE module in slot 2 of the remote ethernet I/P  rack]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[COP(remote_enbt:2:I,SOE_DataTemp[2],1) ,MOV(SOE_LastDataValue[2],remote_enbt:2:O.EventAck) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="SOE_ExtractFifo" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Copy the new SOE module number, name and data to the next storage array

Note, SOE_Next is used as the indirect address of the module number thru out this routine.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(SOE_Next,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData[SOE_StoreIndex].Module_Number) ,COP(SOE_ModuleNames[SOE_Next],SOE_Fifo_ExtractData[SOE_StoreIndex].Module_Name,1) ,COP(SOE_DataTemp[SOE_Next].Fault,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData[SOE_StoreIndex].Fault,11) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Add one to the data storage index so that next time thru this code, data is moved into the next available slot.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[ADD(1,SOE_StoreIndex,SOE_StoreIndex);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
This routine will store 2000 SOE events.  
After 2000, the SOE_LocalBufferFull indicator is set and must be cleared by the user uploading data.

Note the individual SOE modules will continue to store an additoinal 160 events after this array fills up.  

If you want more than 2000 events captured, change the size of the storage array and this GRT instruction.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GRT(SOE_StoreIndex,1999)OTE(SOE_LocalBufferFull);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Move the current event number into the LastData array.  This will than be ACKed to the module the next time the AliasData routine is executed.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(SOE_DataTemp[SOE_Next].EventNumber,SOE_LastDataValue[SOE_Next]);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="SOE_InsertionSort" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[INSERTION SORT
This routine performs an insertion sort on an array of data.  Input parameters include the number of items to Sort, and the actual data to sort.  Output is a 2000 element array of the sorted data.  It is about twice as fast as a traditional bubble sort.  


]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[SBR(Sort_NumToSort,Sort_ArrayIn)NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Copy the input array into the sorting area

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[COP(Sort_ArrayIn[0],Sort_ArrayOut[0],Sort_NumToSort);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Start sorting at element 1

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(1,Sort_I);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Start of outer loop

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(I_loop)NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
copy element i of the array into temp storage

move current i value into J
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[COP(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_I],Sort_Temp,1) ,MOV(Sort_I,Sort_J) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Start of Inner Loop

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(J_loop)NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Inspect the upper 32 bits of CST.  If element J - 1 is greater than element copied into temp , switch places with the copy.

The CST is a full 64 bit number (two 32 bit numbers).  The .31 boolean operations in this rung are to handle the negative bit (.31) going high in the time.  The controller basicaly counts to 2147483647 after which all time's appear negative because bit .31 turns on.  We use the .31st bit here to identify either the sign of the data is the same, i.e. both negative or both positive, in which case use the GRT instruction to determin order.  Or if J-1 is negative and Temp is positive, it means J-1 is really a greater time than temp and perform the copy.   

The Jump stops further inspections of this pair and goes to the next J

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GRT(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J-1].CSTTimeStamp[1],Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[1]) [XIC(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[1].31) XIC(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[1].31) ,XIO(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[1].31) XIO(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[1].31) ] ,XIC(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[1].31) XIO(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[1].31) ][COP(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J-1],Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J],1) ,JMP(NextJ) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[


Inspect the lower 32 bits of CST.  If element J - 1 is greater than element copied into temp , switch places with the copy.

The CST is a full 64 bit number (two 32 bit numbers).  The .31 boolean operations in this rung are to handle the negative bit (.31) going high in the time.  The controller basicaly counts to 2147483647 after which all time's appear negative because bit .31 turns on.  We use the .31st bit here to identify either the sign of the data is the same, i.e. both negative or both positive, in which case use the GRT instruction to determin order.  Or if J-1 is negative and Temp is positive, it means J-1 is really a greater time than temp and perform the copy.   


The Jump stops further inspections of this pair and goes to the next J

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GRT(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J-1].CSTTimeStamp[0],Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[0]) [XIC(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[0].31) XIC(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[0].31) ,XIO(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[0].31) XIO(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[0].31) ] ,XIC(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J- 1].CSTTimeStamp[0].31) XIO(Sort_Temp.CSTTimeStamp[0].31) ][COP(Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J-1],Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J],1) ,JMP(NextJ) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Move data out of temp

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(NextI)COP(Sort_Temp,Sort_ArrayOut[Sort_J],1);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Add one to I and loop back to re-run both inner and outer loops

If I is indexed up to equal the sort size, finish and exit the routine.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[ADD(Sort_I,1,Sort_I) ,LES(Sort_I,Sort_NumToSort) JMP(I_loop) ,JMP(Sort_Done) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Subtract 1 from J and loop back to do the next compair.

When J goes to zero, get next I.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(NextJ)[SUB(Sort_J,1,Sort_J) ,GRT(Sort_J,0) JMP(J_loop) ,JMP(NextI) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Sort is done and exit the routine.

Return the array of sorted data.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(Sort_Done)RET(Sort_ArrayOut);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="SOE_MainRoutine" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
All information provided  - No Warranty or implied merchantability. 
Refer to the RSLogix 5000 End User License Agreement (EULA) in the Release Notes

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This program is an example of extracting data from an SOE system (multiple modules), with the following constraints:

Modules configured in CST_FifoMode. 
The Retrieve By Point mode bit is not enabled in the module output word.

To use this sample you must perform the following:

-  Set the tag SOE_NumModules equal to the number of SOE modules you wish to extract event data from (note arrays are currently dimensioned to support a max of 10 SOE modules. If your system has more than 10, you will need to increase the array sizes on SOE_DataTemp, SOE_LastDataValue, and SOE_ModuleNames)

-  Set the string array, SOE_ModuleNames, to give each module a unique name so it can be identified later.

-  In the SOE_AliasData routine, create/modify one rung for each SOE module in the system.

-Set SOE_ClearHistory to empty the stored data and reset the fill pointer

- Set SOE_SortDataNow to sort the stored data in time relative order]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
This rung jumps to the alias data routine to move all SOE data into a temporary storage array.  This is done to allow easy manipulation, via indirect addressing, by the Extract Fifo routine.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(SOE_AliasData,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung changes which SOE module the ExtractFifo routine is looking at by counting from zero to 1 less than the value set in number of SOE modules.  The parameter SOE_Next is then used as an indirect address into the different SOE modules via SOE_AliasData.   ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[ADD(SOE_Next,1,SOE_Next) ,GEQ(SOE_Next,SOE_NumModules) MOV(0,SOE_Next) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Test to see if there is data not yet stored by the ExtractFifo routine, in the input structure for the SOE module currently being analyzed.  A value different than the last acquired data in the event number will cause the extractFifo routine to be called. 
Note the second NEQ tests for a value of zero which indicates that no data is currently in the SOE modules buffer.  
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NEQ(SOE_DataTemp[SOE_Next].EventNumber,SOE_LastDataValue[SOE_Next])NEQ(SOE_DataTemp[SOE_Next].EventNumber,0)JSR(SOE_ExtractFifo,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[
Run this rung after all SOE Data is copied from the controller to the HMI or data storage area.

This rung clears the storage areas on the controller for Fifo_ExtractData, FifioExtractDataSorted, and resets the pointer for these arrays to zero.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(SOE_ClearHistory)[FLL(0,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData[0],2000) ,FLL(0,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted[0],2000) ,MOV(0,SOE_StoreIndex) ,OTU(SOE_ClearHistory) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[When we are ready to sort, we can copy myarray to another array called sorted array and use a structured text routine to perform a bubble sort on it

IMPORTANT:  Note this sorting operation takes approx 600ms to sort 1000 tags.  Do not run this sort routine when time critical machine operations are required.  In the event that you can not aford to use this much CPU time for sorting, we recomend uploading this array and sorting it on your HMI or data server PC.  ]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(SOE_SortDataNow)[JSR(SOE_InsertionSort,2,NunToSort,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData,SOE_Fifo_ExtractData_sorted) ,OTU(SOE_SortDataNow) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
</Program>
